4.1 Starting transcription from Emacs
=====================================

Two commands launch transcription:

   • ‘M-x eve-transcribe’ prompts for a directory, scans for media
     files, and starts ‘eve transcribe’ asynchronously.  While it runs,
     Eve shows a visible ‘Eve: transcribing…’ mode-line status.  On
     success the manifest opens automatically in eve-mode.

   • ‘M-x eve-dired-transcribe’ runs from Dired using the marked files
     (or the file at point when nothing is marked).

   On failure, the ‘*eve transcribe*’ buffer appears so you can inspect
the error output.


File: eve.info,  Node: Timing and output validity,  Next: Backends,  Prev: Starting transcription from Emacs,  Up: Transcription

4.2 Timing and output validity
==============================

The transcriber writes v1.1 with a per-source audio sample clock,
canonical spoken words, and tick intervals.  Backend precision is
retained through segmentation and converted once to nearest
source-sample ticks.  The 16 kHz ASR working audio preserves leading
media-time offsets.

   New manifests explicitly keep fillers and set
‘render.preserve_short_gaps’ to ‘0’.  Change these saved render settings
in the raw JSON companion when needed; they are authoritative for v1.1
rendering.

   Missing, reversed, zero-duration, or sub-tick-collapsed word
intervals block publication of a successful transcription.  Eve does not
add a tick or stretch neighbouring words to make validation pass.
Existing output is left untouched on validation failure.  Default
transcription preserves ASR estimates without certifying acoustic
accuracy.  The CLI's opt-in ‘--word-aligner ctc’ path uses unchanged
native CTC estimates after English faster-whisper recognition; no human
timing annotations are needed.  Unsigned English integers, including
grouped thousands and sentence punctuation, use alignment-only
verbalization.  ‘11,’ remains the same token and spelling while CTC
matches ‘ELEVEN’.  Multiword readings remain atomic.  Four-digit numbers
compare cardinal and year readings against the same acoustic emissions,
rather than assuming a year pronunciation.  Selected readings and the
pinned normalizer's version are saved in the alignment audit.  Ambiguous
score ties or more than 32 reading paths fail without guessing.
Decimals, signed/currency/percentage forms, leading zeros, numeric
compounds and ordinals remain unsupported.

   Experimental backend status does not require force.  A recognizer/CTC
boundary disagreement greater than 160 ms is recorded in
‘alignment.diagnostics’ without blocking publication: disagreement alone
does not establish that CTC is wrong.  Missing or invalid recognizer
timing used only for comparison is also a non-blocking
‘recognizer_timing_unavailable’ diagnostic when independent native
alignment passes structural validation.  The report retains the original
comparison boundaries as strings, or null for missing values, including
non-finite values without invalid JSON.  This does not repair either
estimate or certify word presence or timing accuracy.  Without an
independent aligner, invalid recognizer word timing remains
authoritative and blocks publication.  ‘--force’ may accept other
recorded quality concerns, never invalid native intervals, changed
tokens, source-bound violations or model errors.  Informational
diagnostics alone never set the recorded ‘forced’ flag, even with
‘--force’.  Selected media envelopes are retained rather than shrunk to
character spans.  If expanded caption envelopes intersect, they are
partitioned jointly between adjacent word intervals, retaining exactly
the same source coverage without repeating footage.  Non-overlapping
captions and intended gaps stay unchanged.  Word estimates, grouping and
order do not change; genuinely overlapping word timings still fail
instead of being repaired.

   Alignment writes a decision report beside the output (or at
‘--alignment-report PATH’).  Successful manifests store the same
‘alignment’ audit record, including model/revision, concerns and any
actual override.  ‘eve text-edit ... --report render.json’ carries that
record into its render report.  Subtitle settings still live in TJM.

   In Emacs, set ‘eve-transcribe-word-aligner’ to ‘"ctc"’ to opt into
this path for ‘M-x eve-transcribe’ and ‘M-x eve-dired-transcribe’.  The
default ‘nil’ keeps backend word timings.  The CTC path requires English
‘faster-whisper’ and the CLI's declared ‘alignment’ dependencies.  Its
progress shows the word alignment stage, and success opens the manifest
as usual.

   A normal run never passes ‘--force’.  Use ‘C-u M-x eve-transcribe’ or
‘C-u M-x eve-dired-transcribe’ to request a quality override for that
run; this requires the CTC opt-in and still cannot override invalid
timing.  A failure shows ‘*eve transcribe*’ and is never retried
automatically.  The alignment audit, including false booleans and empty
arrays, survives editor saving and undo.  Empty segment tag lists also
remain arrays in saved and render manifests.  CTC completion skips
legacy suspicious-region ASR repair offers; the word-aligner setting
does not change manual region retranscription commands.

   The pause diagnostic is provisional and non-blocking: Silero-VAD gaps
of at least 250 ms, with 80 ms excluded at each edge.  Representative
validation is still needed before it becomes a blocking gate.  Runtime
completion does not certify the separate release target of 95% of
reference boundaries within 80 ms (40 ms stretch), nor the operational
target of 95% normal completion.


File: eve.info,  Node: Backends,  Next: Configuration,  Prev: Timing and output validity,  Up: Transcription

4.3 Backends
============

‘eve transcribe’ supports three backends:

Backend            Flag                         Notes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘faster-whisper’   ‘--backend faster-whisper’   Default.  Fast, local inference.
‘transformers’     ‘--backend transformers’     HuggingFace models.  Needs optional deps.
‘nemo’             ‘--backend nemo’             NVIDIA NeMo.  Needs optional deps.

   The ‘--verbatim’ flag (on by default in Emacs) biases
‘faster-whisper’ toward preserving disfluencies and filler words.  In
verbatim mode, faster-whisper also receives default filler hotwords such
as ‘um’, ‘uh’, ‘like’, and ‘you know’; set ‘eve-transcribe-hotwords’ or
pass ‘--hotwords’ to override them.  For ‘transformers’ and ‘nemo’,
choose a verbatim-friendly model instead.

   For an explicitly supplied local CTranslate2 model, the CLI also
supports an experimental ‘--recognition-profile trained-verbatim’ mode:

     eve transcribe input.mp4 --output edit.tjm.json \
       --recognition-profile trained-verbatim --model /path/local-ct2-model \
       --device cpu --word-aligner ctc

   This fixes English CPU/int8 decoding at beam 5, with VAD, temperature
zero, no previous-text conditioning, and no initial prompt or hotwords.
It does not change defaults, download or convert recognition weights, or
certify quality.  The local directory must contain ‘model.bin’,
‘config.json’, ‘tokenizer.json’ and ‘preprocessor_config.json’ and
‘vocabulary.json’.  Actual file hashes and the experimental profile are
recorded in ‘alignment.recognition’; hashes do not certify upstream
provenance.  The existing native aligner retains its own pinned
model/cache and offline policy; set ‘HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1
TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1’ to forbid model downloads.

   Unchanged recognizer text supplies whole-word identity: ‘I'M’ is one
timed token, even when the SDK fragments it into ‘I’ and ‘'M’.  Exact
character mapping is required; ambiguous mapping fails rather than
correcting text.  Native CTC independently estimates the whole-word
interval; fragment timings are never stitched.  Original SDK text,
fragment boundaries and mappings are retained in
‘alignment.sources[].recognition_segments’.  Caption splitting occurs
afterward, without changing word times or losing selected media/gaps.
Existing outputs survive failures.  See CLI documentation
(../cli/README.md) for the tested candidate and revision.

   For full-source Emacs commands, including ‘eve-dired-transcribe’,
select the recognition profile independently of the backend and model:

     (let ((eve-transcribe-backend "faster-whisper")
           (eve-transcribe-model "/path/local-ct2-model")
           (eve-transcribe-recognition-profile "trained-verbatim")
           (eve-transcribe-word-aligner "ctc")
           (eve-transcribe-verbatim t)
           (eve-transcribe-hotwords nil))
       (call-interactively #'eve-dired-transcribe))

   Alternatively, customize those variables for subsequent runs.  The
profile does not select a checkpoint: another compatible local model
needs only a different ‘eve-transcribe-model’ value, not a new
model-specific mode or registry entry.  A different backend/decoding
policy still needs explicit CLI/frontend support and separate quality
evidence.  Unsupported combinations fail before launch; there is no
automatic model switch, fallback or force.  The selected profile is
captured per job and recorded by the CLI in the alignment audit.  The
default profile keeps existing command behavior.  Non-default profiles
are not supported for region retranscription and are rejected rather
than silently using another policy.  Literal casing and contractions are
preserved; display rewriting is never a required or automatic
transcription step.

   Choose recognition and alignment models whose published licenses
permit the intended use of both their weights and generated outputs.
This is a non-commercial project; non-commercial research models may be
evaluated here.  Check model and output terms, not only the inference
code's license, and verify production or operational deployment rights
separately before routine use.

   Licensing eligibility and backend availability do not establish
verbatim recall or timing accuracy.  Model quality must be qualified
separately; a backend's ‘--verbatim’ option or a model's name is not a
guarantee of filler preservation.


File: eve.info,  Node: Configuration,  Next: Installing optional backends,  Prev: Backends,  Up: Transcription

4.4 Configuration
=================

Variable                               Default               Description
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘eve-transcribe-backend’               ‘"faster-whisper"’    Backend name passed to ‘eve transcribe’.
‘eve-transcribe-model’                 ‘"medium.en"’         Model name/path, independent of recognition profile.
‘eve-transcribe-recognition-profile’   ‘"default"’           Full-source decoding/token policy; ‘"trained-verbatim"’ requires native CTC and a compatible local model.
‘eve-transcribe-word-aligner’          ‘nil’                 Set to ‘"ctc"’ for opt-in native word alignment on full-source transcription.
‘eve-transcribe-verbatim’              ‘t’                   Pass ‘--verbatim’ to the backend.
‘eve-transcribe-hotwords’              ‘nil’                 Optional hotwords override; nil uses CLI defaults.
‘eve-transcribe-tag-fillers’           ‘t’                   Tag filler words during transcription.
‘eve-media-extensions’                 ‘("mp4" "mov" ...)’   File types scanned by ‘eve-transcribe’.


