5.2 Deletion and restoration
============================

   • ‘d’ (‘eve-delete-word’) — marks the word at point as deleted.
     Deleted words are hidden in the buffer (‘eve-hide-deleted-mode’)
     and excluded from compilation.  The operation is non-destructive:
     the word remains in the JSON and can be restored.

   • ‘D’ (‘eve-delete-segment’) — marks the entire current segment as
     deleted.

   Deleted content is shown or hidden with ‘C-c C-h’
(‘eve-hide-deleted-mode’).


File: eve.info,  Node: Splitting and merging,  Next: Reordering,  Prev: Deletion and restoration,  Up: Editing

5.3 Splitting and merging
=========================

   • ‘s’ or ‘|’ (‘eve-split-segment’) — splits the current segment at
     point, creating two segments with the text and timing divided at
     the cut.  In the segment panel, the word under point takes
     precedence over older keyboard focus.  Modal keymaps can override
     ‘s’; ‘|’ or ‘M-x eve-segment-panel-split’ invokes the panel split
     command directly.

   • ‘m’ or ‘)’ (‘eve-merge-with-next’) — merges the current segment
     with the one below, combining text and adjusting the end time.

   For v1.1, splitting partitions the original source interval at an
existing word edge without retiming words or losing retained footage.
Merging preserves word records and refreshes the combined caption.
Different source clocks, backwards/overlapping source intervals, and
mixed timed/untimed captions cannot be forced into one timed group.
Reordering remains valid without changing any source-local ticks.

   *Regroup first, then delete:* v1.1 split/merge commands reject groups
with active word cuts, including fillers removed by
‘render.filler_policy = "drop"’.  Restore the cuts before regrouping,
then reapply them.  A filler mark with policy ‘"keep"’ is not an active
cut; explicit non-filler decisions remain respected.  Merging also
refuses to span a deleted group, including one hidden between the
selected groups.  Force cannot bypass these checks.  This prevents the
renderer’s per-group trimming rules from silently reinstating omitted
footage; rejection leaves both data and undo history unchanged.


File: eve.info,  Node: Reordering,  Next: Markers and sections,  Prev: Splitting and merging,  Up: Editing

5.4 Reordering
==============

   • ‘J’ (‘eve-move-segment-down’) — move the current segment one
     position down.
   • ‘K’ (‘eve-move-segment-up’) — move the current segment one position
     up.
   • ‘y’ (‘eve-copy-segment’) — copy the current segment or marker,
     including edit metadata such as b-roll.
   • ‘C-y’ (‘eve-paste-segment’) — paste the copied segment after point.
     Pasting into another TJM buffer also copies the referenced source
     descriptor when needed.


File: eve.info,  Node: Markers and sections,  Next: Inline editing,  Prev: Reordering,  Up: Editing

5.5 Markers and sections
========================

Press ‘o’ (‘eve-insert-marker’) to insert a marker above the current
segment.  Markers act as named section headings.  They appear in the
buffer as bold titles.

   When you compile with ‘C-c C-c’ while point is on a marker, the
marker and the segments in that section are compiled.  Plain markers are
structural headings and render no media, but a marker with ‘edit.broll’
is included as a timeline unit.  This is useful for intro cards, outro
cards, interstitial title cards, and for working on one chapter of a
longer video.


File: eve.info,  Node: Inline editing,  Next: Metadata,  Prev: Markers and sections,  Up: Editing

5.6 Inline editing
==================

Press ‘e’ (‘eve-dwim-edit’) to edit the text or metadata of the segment
at point.  On a marker it edits the title.  For timed v1.1 segments it
changes ‘display’ fragments, never the factual ‘spoken’ tokens or their
source ticks.  The panel's ‘i’ action edits one focused token or the
selected token range; ‘E’ edits the segment's token displays.

   A single v1.1 token can display a complete phrase, which highlights
and deletes as one unit.  For a bulk replacement, supply one replacement
word per selected timed token; otherwise edit the tokens individually.
Ambiguous replacements are rejected before changing data or undo
history.  Capitalization and punctuation actions also change only token
displays.  Karaoke caption text is kept synchronized with surviving
token displays after word edits.

   The legacy seconds-only time editor and local transcript regeneration
are not available for v1.1: they fail without changing data rather than
silently rewriting aliases or losing exact clocks.  Explicit source-tick
changes can be made through the raw JSON view.  Existing-timeline
editing and export do not require transcription or timing review.


File: eve.info,  Node: Metadata,  Next: Undo and redo,  Prev: Inline editing,  Up: Editing

5.7 Metadata
============

Key         Command                Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘t’         ‘eve-toggle-tag’       Toggle a tag on the segment at point.
‘i’         ‘eve-edit-speaker’     Set the speaker label for the segment.
‘r’         ‘eve-edit-start-end’   Edit legacy v1 timecodes; rejected safely for v1.1.
‘C-c C-n’   ‘eve-edit-notes’       Edit free-form notes for the segment.


File: eve.info,  Node: Undo and redo,  Next: Validation,  Prev: Metadata,  Up: Editing

5.8 Undo and redo
=================

   • ‘u’ (‘eve-undo’) — undo the last edit.
   • ‘C-r’ (‘eve-redo’) — redo a previously undone edit.

   Eve maintains its own undo stack separate from Emacs's built-in undo.


File: eve.info,  Node: Validation,  Prev: Undo and redo,  Up: Editing

5.9 Validation
==============

Eve validates the manifest automatically on every save (controlled by
‘eve-validation-on-save’).  Manual validation runs with ‘C-c C-v’
(‘eve-validate’).  Timing inconsistencies are reported in the echo area.


