7.1 Concept
===========

B-roll is supplementary footage that plays over (or instead of) the main
recording.  Eve supports three compositing modes:

   • *Replace* (default) — the b-roll clip completely replaces the
     source video for the duration of the segment.  The source audio is
     kept unless you change the audio policy.

   • *Pip* (picture-in-picture) — the b-roll is scaled down and
     composited on top of the source video.  You control position and
     size.

   • *Overlay* — alias for replace.  Treated identically by the
     renderer.


File: eve.info,  Node: Editing b-roll metadata,  Next: Compositing modes in detail,  Prev: Concept (1),  Up: B-Roll

7.2 Editing b-roll metadata
===========================

Press ‘b’ (‘eve-edit-broll’) on a segment to set or modify its b-roll.
The command prompts for each field in turn:

Field            Prompt                              Description
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘file’           "B-roll file"                       Path to a video, image, or ‘.json’ template.  Blank clears the b-roll.
‘mode’           "Mode (overlay/replace/pip)"        Compositing mode (see above).
‘audio’          "Audio (source/mute/broll)"         Which audio track the rendered segment uses.
‘start_offset’   "Start offset (MM:SS or seconds)"   Where to begin playback in the b-roll clip.
‘duration’       "Duration (MM:SS or seconds)"       How long the b-roll plays; blank uses the segment duration.
‘still’          "Treat as still image?"             Auto-detected for ‘.png’, ‘.jpg’, etc.  Loops the image for the segment.
‘continue’       "Continue from previous?"           Part of a continuation chain (see below).

   Additional keys:

Key         Command                         Description
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘B’         ‘eve-toggle-broll-continue’     Copy b-roll from the previous segment and mark as continuation.
‘C-c C-b’   ‘eve-edit-broll-placeholders’   Edit placeholder values for the current segment.

   B-roll metadata is stored in the segment's ‘edit.broll’ field in the
JSON manifest.


File: eve.info,  Node: Compositing modes in detail,  Next: Audio policy,  Prev: Editing b-roll metadata,  Up: B-Roll

7.3 Compositing modes in detail
===============================

* Menu:

* Replace mode::
* Pip mode::


File: eve.info,  Node: Replace mode,  Next: Pip mode,  Up: Compositing modes in detail

7.3.1 Replace mode
------------------

The b-roll video replaces the source video entirely.  The renderer trims
the b-roll to the segment's duration, starting at ‘start_offset’.  If
the b-roll is shorter than the segment, it is extended to fit.

   When ‘still’ is true, the image is looped for the full segment
duration using ‘ffmpeg -loop 1’.


File: eve.info,  Node: Pip mode,  Prev: Replace mode,  Up: Compositing modes in detail

7.3.2 Pip mode
--------------

The b-roll is scaled and positioned as an inset window over the source.
Position and size are controlled by a ‘position’ object in the JSON:

     {
       "file": "demo-recording.mp4",
       "mode": "pip",
       "position": {
         "x": 0.05,
         "y": 0.05,
         "width": 0.3
       }
     }

Field     Default   Description
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘x’       ‘0.05’    Horizontal offset as a fraction of the main video width (0.0-1.0).
‘y’       ‘0.05’    Vertical offset as a fraction of the main video height (0.0-1.0).
‘width’   ‘0.3’     Width of the pip window as a fraction of the main video width.

   The height is calculated automatically to preserve the b-roll's
aspect ratio.


File: eve.info,  Node: Audio policy,  Next: JSON templates,  Prev: Compositing modes in detail,  Up: B-Roll

7.4 Audio policy
================

Each b-roll entry has an ‘audio’ field that controls which audio track
appears in the rendered segment:

   • ‘source’ (default) — keep the original recording's audio.
   • ‘broll’ — use the audio track from the b-roll clip.  Not available
     for still images.
   • ‘mute’ — no audio for this segment.


File: eve.info,  Node: JSON templates,  Next: Placeholders without templates,  Prev: Audio policy,  Up: B-Roll

7.5 JSON templates
==================

When the ‘file’ field points to a ‘.json’ file instead of a media file,
eve treats it as a *template*.  The template JSON has this structure:

     {
       "template": "lower-third.png",
       "overlays": [
         {
           "placeholder": "SPEAKER_NAME",
           "fontsize": 36,
           "color": "white",
           "x": 80,
           "y": 620,
           "boxColor": "black@0.6"
         },
         {
           "placeholder": "SPEAKER_TITLE",
           "fontsize": 24,
           "color": "#cccccc",
           "x": 80,
           "y": 665
         }
       ],
       "placeholders": {
         "SPEAKER_NAME": "Full Name",
         "SPEAKER_TITLE": "Job Title"
       }
     }

   • ‘template’ — path to the actual media file (image or video).
   • ‘overlays’ — a list of text overlays rendered with ffmpeg
     ‘drawtext’.  Each overlay references a ‘placeholder’ key by name.
   • ‘placeholders’ — default values for each placeholder.
     Segment-level overrides (set via ‘C-c C-b’) take precedence.

* Menu:

* Overlay fields::
* Concrete example lower-third title card::


File: eve.info,  Node: Overlay fields,  Next: Concrete example lower-third title card,  Up: JSON templates

7.5.1 Overlay fields
--------------------

Each overlay in the ‘overlays’ array supports:

Field                                     Description
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘placeholder’                             Name of the placeholder key this overlay renders.
‘fontsize’ (or ‘size’)                    Font size in pixels.
‘color’ (or ‘fontColor’)                  Font colour (ffmpeg colour syntax: name, ‘#rrggbb’, ‘white@0.8’).
‘x’ (or ‘position_x’)                     Horizontal position (pixels or ffmpeg expression like ‘(w-text_w)/2’).
‘y’ (or ‘position_y’)                     Vertical position (pixels or ffmpeg expression).
‘align’                                   Set to ‘"center"’ to auto-centre horizontally and vertically.
‘font’ (or ‘fontfile’)                    Path to a ‘.ttf’ or ‘.otf’ font file.
‘boxColor’ (or ‘box_color’)               Background box colour (e.g.  ‘black@0.6’ for 60% opacity).
‘shadowColor’ / ‘shadow_x’ / ‘shadow_y’   Drop shadow parameters.
‘start’ / ‘end’ / ‘duration’              Time window (seconds) during which the overlay is visible.


File: eve.info,  Node: Concrete example lower-third title card,  Prev: Overlay fields,  Up: JSON templates

7.5.2 Concrete example: lower-third title card
----------------------------------------------

Suppose you have a background image ‘lower-third.png’ and you want to
show the speaker's name and title during a segment.

  1. Create the template file ‘assets/lower-third.json’:

          {
            "template": "assets/lower-third.png",
            "overlays": [
              {
                "placeholder": "NAME",
                "fontsize": 42,
                "color": "white",
                "x": 80,
                "y": 620,
                "font": "assets/Inter-Bold.ttf",
                "boxColor": "black@0.5"
              },
              {
                "placeholder": "ROLE",
                "fontsize": 28,
                "color": "#cccccc",
                "x": 80,
                "y": 672,
                "font": "assets/Inter-Regular.ttf"
              }
            ],
            "placeholders": {
              "NAME": "Speaker Name",
              "ROLE": "Speaker Role"
            }
          }

  2. In eve-mode, press ‘b’ on the segment where you want the title
     card.  Set ‘file’ to ‘assets/lower-third.json’.  Set ‘mode’ to
     ‘replace’.

  3. Press ‘C-c C-b’ to edit placeholders for this segment:

          Placeholder NAME (default Speaker Name): Jane Doe
          Placeholder ROLE (default Speaker Role): Product Lead
          New placeholder key (blank to finish):

  4. Compile with ‘C-c C-c’.  The rendered segment shows
     ‘lower-third.png’ with "Jane Doe" and "Product Lead" burned in as
     text overlays at the specified positions and sizes.

   Each segment can override different placeholder values from the same
template.  The template is reused; only the text changes.


File: eve.info,  Node: Placeholders without templates,  Next: Continuation chains,  Prev: JSON templates,  Up: B-Roll

7.6 Placeholders without templates
==================================

You can also set placeholder key-value pairs directly on a segment
without a JSON template, using ‘C-c C-b’.  These are stored in the
manifest under ‘edit.broll.placeholders’ and are available for any
overlay definitions.


File: eve.info,  Node: Continuation chains,  Prev: Placeholders without templates,  Up: B-Roll

7.7 Continuation chains
=======================

When a single b-roll clip should span multiple consecutive segments, use
continuation chains:

  1. Press ‘b’ on the first segment and configure the b-roll normally.
  2. Move to the next segment and press ‘B’
     (‘eve-toggle-broll-continue’).

   ‘B’ copies the b-roll metadata from the nearest preceding segment
that has b-roll, then auto-calculates:

   • ‘continue’: set to ‘true’.
   • ‘start_offset’: previous offset + previous segment duration, so
     playback continues seamlessly from where the last segment left off.
   • ‘duration’: remaining duration of the b-roll clip (if the original
     had a duration set).

   Repeat ‘B’ on each subsequent segment in the chain.  The renderer
treats the entire chain as one continuous b-roll playback — the clip
advances smoothly across segment boundaries without restarting.


File: eve.info,  Node: Compilation and Rendering,  Next: Playback,  Prev: B-Roll,  Up: Top

