8.1 Two modes: draft and final
==============================

‘C-c C-c’ (‘eve-compile’) compiles the manifest at draft quality — fast
enough to catch mistakes before committing to a full export.

   ‘C-u C-c C-c’ compiles at final quality (H.264 medium/CRF 18) for
delivery.

   The quality used can be changed permanently with
‘eve-compile-quality’.


File: eve.info,  Node: Burned-in captions,  Next: Under the hood,  Prev: Two modes draft and final,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.2 Burned-in captions
======================

An existing valid v1.1 timeline can be opened and exported directly; no
new transcription or alignment is required.  Eve reads source-local
ticks using each source's timebase, even without legacy ‘start’, ‘end’,
‘text’ or ‘token’ aliases.  Displayed seconds are a view: opening,
configuring subtitles and saving do not round or replace the
authoritative ticks.  Existing ‘word.display’ fragments appear together
as one timed token, including multiword fragments; spoken text remains
separate.

   Run ‘M-x eve-enable-subtitle-burning’ in a v1.1 TJM buffer or its
context panel, then save with ‘C-x C-s’ or compile normally.  The
command copies ‘eve-subtitle-defaults’ into the manifest's optional
‘subtitles’ object; the resulting video follows those saved settings,
not the current Emacs session.  Existing subtitle settings are left
unchanged, including extension fields.  Legacy v1 does not support
burning: supply a valid v1.1 timeline instead.  Changing only the
version field does not add the required clock/tick data.

   The defaults use word highlighting, bold 144-pixel white text, a
3-pixel black outline, and a gold active token.  Captions are
bottom-centered with a 180-pixel margin, scaled from a 1920×1080
reference canvas.  The large text-first profile is intentional: speech
and captions can dominate the video rather than serve as a small
annotation.  On a 1080×1920 portrait export, uniform scaling gives an
81-pixel font and a 101.25-pixel bottom margin.  One TJM segment is one
caption; pauses leave the caption visible without highlighting, and
captions disappear between segments.

   The requested font must be installed.  For example, to use DejaVu
Sans for manifests that do not yet have subtitle settings:

     (with-eval-after-load 'eve
       (setf (alist-get 'family (alist-get 'font eve-subtitle-defaults))
             "DejaVu Sans"))

   Customize the complete default profile with ‘M-x customize-variable
RET eve-subtitle-defaults RET’.  Its values are JSON-compatible alists:
‘t’ is true, ‘:json-false’ is false, and ‘nil’ is null.  To use plain
captions instead of highlighting, set its ‘style’ member to ‘"plain"’.

   Run ‘M-x eve-disable-subtitle-burning’ to remove the manifest's
subtitle object.  Enabling/disabling is undoable with Eve's normal undo
command.  Changing Emacs defaults never replaces settings already saved
in a TJM file; to deliberately replace them, disable burning and then
enable it again with the new defaults.  These commands do not change
media selections, token timings, or optional embedded subtitle tracks.
Burning re-encodes video; audio timing is preserved.

   Fractional cuts retain native frame phase, including at 29.97 fps.
The first video frame may legitimately have a nonzero presentation time
when the source cut falls between frames.  Encoding, embedded-track
muxing and burning preserve that phase rather than rounding it onto a
new frame-rate grid.  Clock-rule changes invalidate older render caches
and resume checkpoints automatically.

   Compilation output appears once in the bottom context view when
context-panels supports buffer adoption, or in one standalone bottom
window otherwise.  Repeated renders keep one log view and preserve focus
in the source buffer.  Render validation failures appear in that log
without automatically opening Python source or the CLI launcher.
Invalid token timings identify the segment, token, and interval that
need repair; missing fonts must be installed or replaced in the manifest
settings.  Eve does not invent timing or silently substitute fonts to
make a render succeed.


File: eve.info,  Node: Under the hood,  Next: Pre-flight validation,  Prev: Burned-in captions,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.3 Under the hood
==================

Eve's rendering pipeline is designed to be fast after the first run:

   • *Quality profiles* — draft uses MJPEG encoding at ultrafast preset;
     final uses H.264 at medium preset.  The codec and preset are
     resolved automatically from the ‘--quality’ flag.

   • *Parallel encoding* — segments are encoded concurrently across all
     available CPU cores (‘eve-compile-jobs’).

   • *Segment cache* — rendered segments are stored in ‘.eve-cache/’
     next to the manifest, keyed by a SHA-256 hash of the segment's
     content and encoding settings.  Unchanged segments are reused; only
     changed ones re-encode.

   • *Speculative pre-rendering* — when ‘eve-auto-render-mode’ is
     enabled, after each save an idle timer
     (‘eve-auto-render-idle-seconds’) fires a low-priority background
     render of the segments that changed.  When you press ‘C-c C-c’, the
     cache is already warm and compilation reduces to a concat step.
     Use ‘M-x eve-render-cache-changed’ to warm changed segments
     immediately, or ‘M-x eve-render-cache-all’ to proactively warm
     every segment cache entry.

   • *Context thumbnails* — timestamp rows in the optional context side
     panel can show cached thumbnails from the rendered output when it
     already exists and is fresh, falling back to source media frames.
     Thumbnail extraction is lazy, visible-row-driven, low-priority, and
     never triggers a full render.


File: eve.info,  Node: Pre-flight validation,  Next: Dry-run analysis,  Prev: Under the hood,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.4 Pre-flight validation
=========================

Before starting a render, eve validates the manifest:

   • All source files must exist and be readable.
   • All b-roll files must exist.
   • Segment timecodes must be non-negative and end after start.
   • Words must fall within their segment bounds.

   Warnings appear in the echo area.  Errors block compilation unless
you confirm.  Run validation explicitly with ‘C-c C-v’ (‘eve-validate’).

   If the estimated render time (from the dry-run analysis) exceeds
‘eve-compile-confirm-threshold’ seconds, eve asks for confirmation.


File: eve.info,  Node: Dry-run analysis,  Next: Checkpoint and resume,  Prev: Pre-flight validation,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.5 Dry-run analysis
====================

Before rendering, eve can report which segments would be re-encoded and
estimate the render time:

     eve text-edit manifest.tjm.json --output out.mp4 --dry-run

   The output (JSON with ‘--json’) includes ‘total_segments’,
‘changed_segments’, and ‘estimated_seconds’.  This information feeds the
pre-flight confirmation prompt.


File: eve.info,  Node: Checkpoint and resume,  Next: Progressive output,  Prev: Dry-run analysis,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.6 Checkpoint and resume
=========================

If a render is interrupted, eve writes a checkpoint file in
‘.eve-cache/’ as segments complete.  The next run picks up from where it
left off: segments already in the cache are reused, and only the
remaining ones are encoded.

   Pass ‘--no-resume’ to force a fresh render from scratch.


File: eve.info,  Node: Progressive output,  Next: Configuration reference,  Prev: Checkpoint and resume,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.7 Progressive output
======================

With ‘--partial-every N’, eve writes a partial ‘<output>.partial.mp4’
after every N segments complete.  A JSON event is emitted on stdout for
each partial file, which Emacs can use to update a live preview.


File: eve.info,  Node: Configuration reference,  Next: CLI reference,  Prev: Progressive output,  Up: Compilation and Rendering

8.8 Configuration reference
===========================

Variable                            Default     Description
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘eve-compile-quality’               ‘"draft"’   ‘"draft"’ for fast preview, ‘"final"’ for delivery.
‘eve-compile-jobs’                  ‘0’         Parallel encode workers; 0 = auto-detect CPU count.
‘eve-compile-scale’                 ‘1.0’       Resolution scale for draft (e.g.  ‘0.5’ = half res).
‘eve-compile-cache’                 ‘t’         Use the ‘.eve-cache/’ segment cache.
‘eve-compile-confirm-threshold’     ‘30’        Estimated seconds above which a confirmation prompt appears.
‘eve-auto-render-default-enabled’   ‘t’         Enable ‘eve-auto-render-mode’ in new Eve buffers.
‘eve-auto-render-idle-seconds’      ‘2.0’       Idle delay before the speculative render fires.
‘eve-auto-render-low-priority’      ‘t’         Run speculative renders via ‘nice -n 19’ when available.
‘eve-auto-render-all-segments’      ‘nil’       Auto-render all segment ids after save instead of only changed ones.
‘eve-context-thumbnails-enabled’    ‘t’         Show thumbnails on context-panel timestamp rows.
‘eve-context-thumbnail-source’      ‘best’      Use rendered thumbnails when available, falling back to source media.
